      "writtenOn": "2026-10-29T20:27:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think indexing from `vm_start` still assumes that `vm_start` maps the start of `pages[0]`. Binder ignores `vm_pgoff`, but an `mmap()` offset of 4KB in a 4KB process sets `vm_slice_off` to 1, and `vm_insert_page()` then maps slice 1 of each page at the address this loop computes (`vma_insert_page_slices()`, aosp/4368056).\n\nThe process would then read every buffer 4KB off. Worse, `vma_insert_page_slices()` maps slice 0 of each page 4KB below that address, and `binder_alloc_free_page()` only zaps the 16KB from the page\u0027s address (taking slice 0 of the next page instead) before `binder_free_page()` frees it. So the process keeps a PTE to a freed page. As far as I can see that already happens before this patch, since `mmap(NULL)` gives a 16KB-aligned `vm_start` either way.\n\nCould `binder_mmap()` reject a non-zero `vma_slice_off(vma)` with `-EINVAL`, as `perf_mmap()` does since aosp/4368055? The helper suggested on `binder_alloc_page_start()` would cover it.",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "a55bca1345dcacbafdc962d5abc851b6f603b918",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
